-- title:
--   Fibre estimate for the chaining ball, integrating in the second endpoint
-- statement:
--   Let $d \ge 1$, let $v \in \mathbb{R}^d$ be a unit vector, let $\vartheta$ satisfy $0 < \vartheta \le \pi/2$, and let $\alpha \ge 0$. For a pair of points $(s,t)$ put
--   $$m(s,t) \;=\; s + \frac{3\lVert s-t\rVert}{\sin\vartheta}\,v \qquad (\texttt{midCentre}),$$
--   the point reached from $s$ by travelling $3\lVert s-t\rVert/\sin\vartheta$ along the axis $v$; the averaging set used in the chaining argument is the closed ball $\bar B\big(m(s,t),\,\|s-t\|\big)$, whose points serve as intermediate points between $s$ and $t$.
--
--   Fix $s, z \in \mathbb{R}^d$. Then, integrating the doubly singular weight over the fibre of the averaging relation above $z$,
--
--   $$\int_{\{t \,:\, z \in \bar B(m(s,t),\|s-t\|)\}} \|s-t\|^{-2d-\alpha}\,dt \;\le\; C_{d,\vartheta,\alpha}\,\|z-s\|^{-d-\alpha}\cdot \omega_d ,$$
--
--   where
--   $$C_{d,\vartheta,\alpha} \;=\; \frac{\bigl(1 + \tfrac{3}{\sin\vartheta}\bigr)^{2d+\alpha}}{\bigl(\tfrac{3}{\sin\vartheta} - 1\bigr)^{d}} \qquad (\texttt{chainConst})$$
--   is an explicit constant depending only on $d$, $\vartheta$ and $\alpha$, and $\omega_d = |\bar B(0,1)|$ is the volume of the unit ball of $\mathbb{R}^d$ (`unitBallVol d`).
--
--   This is the estimate that survives the Tonelli exchange in the chaining argument, and it is the reason that argument is scale invariant: the weight $\|s-t\|^{-2d-\alpha}$, integrated over the $t$-fibre above an intermediate point $z$, comes back as $\|z-s\|^{-d-\alpha}$, which is exactly the weight of the fractional form on the pair $(s,z)$ — the quantity that the lower kernel bound then converts into $k(s,z)$.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** Values are in `ℝ≥0∞` and the powers are transported by `ENNReal.At $z = s$ the right-hand side is **$0$, not $+\infty$**: the exponent $-d-\alpha$ is negative and `Real.rpow` sends $0$ to $0$ there. So that case is not a triviality but the sharpest instance, asserting that the fibre integral vanishes — which holds because the fibre collapses to the single point $\{s\}$, a null set. The exponent $-2d-\alpha$ is written $-(2d)-\alpha$ with a real cast of $d$; in $C_{d,\vartheta,\alpha}$ the numerator's exponent $2d+\alpha$ is likewise a real exponent while the denominator's $d$ is a natural power.
